WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.999
Cesar Garcia and his wife Claudia have lived
in this home

00:00:02.999 --> 00:00:06.940
in the desert town of Coachella, California
for more than 13 years.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:12.240
They’ve raised three kids there, and Claudia
runs an at-home daycare out of the house.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.660
But three years ago, they made a mistake that
could cost them their home and livelihood:

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:20.380
when converting their back porch into a TV
room,

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.320
they neglected to get a building permit from
the city.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.440
The person I hired you know they do a quote,

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.180
only with no permit from the city of Coachella.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.100
Garcia ignored early warnings from the city.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.340
Then a team of inspectors showed up unannounced.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.380
He pleaded guilty in court, paid a $900 fine,

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.380
and demolished the structure as ordered by
the judge.

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:47.140
Then he got the proper permit before rebuilding
and upgrading it into a room for the daycare.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.200
He thought the issue was resolved.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.300
But six months later a bill arrived...for
$26,000.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.820
As the local newspaper the Desert Sun first
reported,

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:03.880
the city of Coachella is holding Garcia responsible
for paying the cost of his own prosecution.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:06.960
The city had hired the private law firm Silver
&amp; Wright.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.720
to prosecute Garcia,

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:14.159
The firm promised the city that its work would
be ““cost neutral or even revenue producing.”

00:01:14.159 --> 00:01:18.740
Garcia, who works as a grocery store manager
about an hour and half from his home,

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:22.920
isn’t the only building code violator to
receive an unexpected bill.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.640
Six months after Ramona Morales paid a $250
fine

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.920
because one of her tenants was illegally housing
chickens,

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.400
Silver and Wright invoiced her for $5,600.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.700
A woman in the neighboring town of Indio

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:40.520
who was cited for putting up illegal Halloween
decorations was charged $2,600.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:44.300
And another family received a bill for $18,500

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:49.280
after pleading guilty to having a broken garage
door and overgrown lawn.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.360
As of November 2017,

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.780
Silver &amp; Wright had collected more than $122,000
in code enforcement fees

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:01.420
from dozens of residents in an area in which
the median annual household income

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.980
is about $36,000.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.920
After Garcia received his bill, he appealed.

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:09.280
That drove his bill up another five grand.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:13.920
Since Garcia couldn’t afford to pay $21,000
in prosecutor’s fees within a month,

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.400
the city put a lein on his house.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.320
It’s like, “You’re going to lose your
house because you’re not paying 21,000.”

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:20.940
This is my house.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.770
This is where we live, this is where we feel safety here.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:28.170
Silver &amp; Wright has a business model that's
premised on cost recovery.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:29.740
They go around to these cities and they say,

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:35.180
“hey, we can get you 90 percent of your
fees or 100 percent of your fees back.”

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.860
Jeffrey Redfern is an attorney with the nonprofit
Institute for Justice,

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:45.380
which has filed a class action lawsuit on
behalf of Garcia and others

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:47.860
charged with the cost of their own prosecution.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.260
This is appealing to the cities because they
figured,

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:53.020
“hey, if they can do this work for free,
that's great.”

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:58.140
The problem is that once the law firm took
over,

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:03.780
they have an incentive to prosecute cases
in a complicated and expensive manner.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.200
So as to drive up the fees that they can collect
from the defendants.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.620
Callahan and Blaine is known and respected

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:14.960
as one of the leading insurance law firms in the nation.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:19.760
Attorney Edward Sousalik is representing Silver
&amp; Wright in the lawsuit.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.520
When situations like this arise, it's the
taxpayers of the city who have to pay for this.

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:31.500
it's absolutely a fair, outcome, because otherwise
who pays that $26,000?

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:34.040
It's the good citizens of the city.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:36.460
The lawsuit claims that the financial arrangement

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:41.040
violates both the Due Process clause of the
14th Amendment to the U.S. Constitution,

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.540
and the California state constitution,

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:49.620
which guarantee the right to a neutral prosecutor
without financial or personal stake in the case.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:54.200
Whether we're talking about civil asset forfeiture
or fines and fees or,

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:59.020
you know, this kind of outsourcing to a private
law firm: It's all really the same thing.

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:00.860
We call it policing for profit.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:07.820
It's turning the criminal justice system into
a tool for raising revenue

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:11.480
That is a naive and false description.

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:14.240
This is a city attorney.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.560
Silver &amp; Wright working to prosecute laws
for the city.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:28.120
They prosecuted over 150 people for Code Violations,
nuisance abatement, nuisance abatement.

00:04:28.129 --> 00:04:31.849
They made the city of safe and healthy place
to live.

00:04:31.849 --> 00:04:37.380
Garcia and the other plaintiffs allege that
Silver &amp; Wright waited six months before billing  them

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:42.120
because after six months, they were no longer
allowed to withdraw their guilty pleas,

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:45.580
which limits their ability to protest the
fees.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:49.420
Is that an intentional strategy on behalf
of Silver and Wright?

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:50.760
Absolutely not.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:53.860
Was he ever informed when he was pleading
guilty

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:57.580
that he was going to have to pay tens of thousands
of dollars of legal fees

00:04:57.580 --> 00:04:59.420
for his own prosecution?

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:03.500
Well, it is certainly my understanding that
the people, when they plead guilty,

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:05.800
they have the sense that they have this knowledge

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:08.200
and we'll depose him thoroughly on this issue.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:11.680
Any criminal defendant is presumed that they
know the law.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:18.640
I mean it's not incumbent upon the police
or the judge or the prosecutor or the city

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:21.940
to tell a criminal defendant what the laws
are.

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:26.180
So what does the law say about charging plaintiffs
for their own prosecution?

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:28.120
Garcia and the other plaintiffs allege

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:34.180
that Silver and Wright aggressively pushed
city officials to change the law.

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:35.580
Before Silver and Wright were hired

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:41.319
there wasn't an ordinance on the books allowing
for cost recovery in these nuisance cases.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:45.260
The city put these ordinances on the books
because silver and right ask them to do so.

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:46.699
One partner at Silver and Wright

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:51.160
is also a vice president of the California
Association of Code Enforcement Officers,

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:54.020
a lobbying organization that works on this
issue,

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:57.580
helping municipalities rewrite their administrative
codes.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:59.260
Is that a conflict of interest?

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:03.840
Well, first off, they don't profit from the
prosecution and the laws.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:04.700
Okay.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:08.300
They are, they are city attorneys who work
for the cities.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.380
And they’re paid for that work, correct?

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:14.999
They do many, many different tasks and responsibilities
for these cities there.

00:06:14.999 --> 00:06:16.600
It's called the city attorney.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:20.140
You handle many different functions and you're
paid by the hour.

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:24.100
In some ways we have a perfect storm for abuse
right now.

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:28.720
We have huge number of very vague laws, lots
of them criminal.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:33.180
Prosecutors who have almost total discretion
to decide how to enforce these laws.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:34.779
They can go after almost anyone they want.

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:38.540
If they're really motivated, they could find
things wrong with my house or your house.

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:43.140
You have the people with that discretion over
very vague, broad laws

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:47.560
who have an interest in getting your money.

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:52.060
At the very least, those people should be
neutral.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:56.520
Outrage over Garcia’s story led to the passage
of a new law in California

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:01.440
prohibiting cities from charging defendants
for their own criminal prosecution.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:05.580
But it doesn’t retroactively absolve Garcia
of his debt.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:09.860
And the lawsuit is also moving forward to
set a legal precedent

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:14.480
that will make other state courts less likely
to tolerate this practice.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:16.920
If we win this case, I think it will go a
long way

00:07:16.929 --> 00:07:20.479
towards establishing standards of prosecutorial
neutrality.

00:07:20.479 --> 00:07:22.899
The people who started writing these codes

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:28.060
never imagined that they would be giving discretion
to enforce them

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:31.100
to people who would then make money off of
them.

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:37.160
As for Garcia, he says the entire ordeal has
put his family’s life on hold.

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:41.280
I still worry about losing it because this
stuff is not done, it's, you know,

00:07:41.289 --> 00:07:45.089
we are still thinking about, you know, those
$31,000.

